    "message": "fix(analytics): stop double-counting cached input tokens\n\nThree defects made the cache figures unusable, all pre-existing.\n\n**The stored input count was our own estimate.** The ledger emits\nmessage_start with a pre-flight estimate, because upstream has not\nreported anything yet; the provider's real prompt_tokens arrives on\nmessage_delta. request_capture read input_tokens only from message_start,\nso every row saved the guess and discarded the truth. Combined with a\nprovider-reported cache figure, that produced 608 rows in a live log where\nthe cached tokens exceeded the entire input -- arithmetically impossible\nfrom one usage payload:\n\n    nvidia_nim  595 rows  tokens_in max 460,340  cache_read max 481,536\n    open_router  13 rows  tokens_in max  79,118  cache_read max  82,176\n\n**Cached tokens were counted twice.** An OpenAI-family prompt_tokens\n*includes* the tokens served from cache. Anthropic's input_tokens excludes\nthem and expects the caller to add cache_read_input_tokens back for the\ntotal. We emitted the first number under the second name, so a warm 268k\nprompt with 261k cached was reported as 529k -- to our own analytics and to\nClaude Code's context and cost display alike. The streaming path now\nsubtracts the read count before emitting input_tokens, so\ninput + cache_read reconstructs prompt_tokens exactly.\n\nDeepSeek falls out of the same rule. It splits prompt_tokens into\nprompt_cache_hit_tokens + prompt_cache_miss_tokens; reporting the hit count\nalone is enough, because the subtraction lands on the miss count. It had\nalso been mapping misses to cache_creation_input_tokens, which counted that\nslice a second time -- a miss is a token the cache did not serve, not a\ntoken written at a premium, which is what Anthropic's field means. Its\nexisting test asserted 30 + 10 + 20 for a 30-token prompt; it now asserts\n20 + 10 and checks the sum.\n\n**Cache writes were never read.** OpenRouter reports the write side as\ncache_write_tokens inside prompt_tokens_details. Nothing read it, so\ncache_write_tokens was null in all 39,000 rows of the live log.\n\nThe dashboard had drifted into two conventions at once: the older helpers\ndivided by tokens_in (OpenAI shape) while the per-request figure added\ncache reads on top (Anthropic shape). Same data, two answers. Everything\nnow uses one definition of total input, and both the summary cards and the\nrequest detail show it alongside the uncached part.\n\nVerification: 18 new tests covering the read/write mapping, zero-versus-\nabsent reporting, the subtraction against real logged numbers, DeepSeek's\nhit/miss split, underflow, and the capture path preferring the provider's\ncount while still keeping the estimate when upstream stays silent. 5/5\nlocal CI; 3119 passed, same 14 failures as the unmodified base.\n\nNot addressed: cache_control is still dropped when converting to the\nOpenAI wire format. Harmless for providers that cache automatically, but it\nmeans caching cannot engage for Anthropic, Qwen or Gemini models routed\nthrough OpenRouter, which require explicit breakpoints. That is a separate\nchange with a wider blast radius.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
